reviewCommentsList.body:
The relaxed `lastRotated` checks do not stop the test, so the Debug abort can still happen.

`LedgerMaster_test.cpp:161`/`:172` and `SHAMapStore_test.cpp:1235`/`:1243` both use non-fatal macros. `BEAST_EXPECT` and `BEAST_EXPECTS` record a failure and return the boolean - they do not leave the testcase. So if either check fails, the test carries on with a bad `lastRotated`.

That matters because `lastRotated` becomes `minSeq`:

- `LedgerMaster_test.cpp:193` - `minSeq = lastRotated;`
- `SHAMapStore_test.cpp:1473` - `minSeq = lastRotated;`

`LedgerIndex` is `std::uint32_t`. With `lastRotated == 0` the loop body then calls:

- `LedgerMaster_test.cpp:216` - `missingFromCompleteLedgerRange(minSeq - 1, maxSeq + 1)` → `first == 0xFFFFFFFF`
- `LedgerMaster_test.cpp:217` - `missingFromCompleteLedgerRange(minSeq - 2, maxSeq - 2)` → `first == 0xFFFFFFFE`
- `SHAMapStore_test.cpp:1510` and `:1512` - the same two calls

`first > last`, so `LedgerMaster.cpp:1595` fires `UNREACHABLE` and a Debug build aborts the whole unit test worker. That is the symptom this PR set out to remove.

`lastRotated == 0` is a real value, not a hypothetical. `getLastRotated()` reads the state DB, and `run()` only writes it at `SHAMapStoreImp.cpp:352-356`, after the store has been handed its first validated ledger. `waitForReady()` already asserts `!store.getLastRotated()` for exactly this reason. `syncStore()` is what now prevents it - but only if the test stops when `syncStore()` reports failure.

The new `if (minSeq + 1 <= maxSeq - 1)` guard does not help here. It protects `(minSeq + 1, maxSeq - 1)`, which collapses when `maxSeq - minSeq < 2`. The abort above comes from `(minSeq - 1, ...)` and `(minSeq - 2, ...)`, which wrap around instead. Those are two different failures and only one is guarded.

Suggested change - make both preconditions fatal, in both files:

```cpp
// LedgerMaster_test.cpp:160-172
if (!BEAST_EXPECT(syncStore(env)))
    return;
LedgerIndex lastRotated = store.getLastRotated();
// ... existing comment ...
if (!BEAST_EXPECTS(lastRotated >= minSeq && lastRotated <= maxSeq, to_string(lastRotated)))
    return;
```

```cpp
// SHAMapStore_test.cpp:1235-1243
if (!BEAST_EXPECT(syncStore(env)))
    return;
// ... existing comment ...
LedgerIndex lastRotated = store.getLastRotated();
if (!BEAST_EXPECTS(
        lastRotated >= minSeq && lastRotated <= maxSeq, std::to_string(lastRotated)))
    return;
```

Both testcases return `void`, so a bare `return;` works. `testLedgerGaps` already uses this idiom elsewhere, for example around the `deleteSeq` checks.

With that in place the underflow is unreachable rather than merely unlikely, and the `if/else` collapse guard becomes belt and braces instead of the only line of defense.

This comment names the wrong mechanism. The store parks in the gating `healthWait()` (SHAMapStoreImp.cpp:362). On Stopping it falls through to the top of `while (true)`, which sets `working_ = false` and notifies before returning. So the untimed `rendezvous()` would return.

This comment is accurate only for `testHealthWaitStopMidRotation`, where run() returns from case `HealthResult::Stopping: return;` at :400 with `working_` still set.

More specifically, `testHealthWaitStop` calls `parkInHealthWait` (`:1677`), which parks the store at the gating health check - the third operand of `readyToRotate` at `SHAMapStoreImp.cpp:361-362`. The store never enters the rotation body, because `readyToRotate` is exactly what gates it at `:388`.

On `stop()`, `healthWait()` returns `Stopping` from `SHAMapStoreImp.cpp:847-848`. Nothing in `run()` inspects that value at this point. `readyToRotate` just becomes `false` by short circuit, `if (readyToRotate)` at `:388` is skipped, and control falls to the bottom of `while (true)` and loops. The next iteration runs `:329-339`:

```cpp
329        healthy_ = true;
...
333            std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(mutex_);
334            working_ = false;
335            rendezvous_.notify_all();
336            if (stop_)
337            {
338                return;
339            }
```

So `working_` is cleared and `rendezvous_` is notified before `run()` returns. An untimed `rendezvous()` after `store.stop()` would return immediately, not block.

The equivalent comment in `testHealthWaitStopMidRotation` (`:1766-1768`) is correct. `parkMidRotation` parks the store inside `clearPrior()`, so `healthWait()` returning `Stopping` reaches the switch at `SHAMapStoreImp.cpp:398-406` and exits through `case HealthResult::Stopping: return;` at `:400-401`, leaving `working_ == true`.

Suggestions:
* Improve the comment here:

```
// stop() sets stop_ and joins the store's thread, so its return is the
// synchronisation point here. It is not instant: the store is sleeping
// out its recovery wait, and only re-tests stop_ once that expires.
//
// No rendezvous() is needed, and none would be wrong either. The store
// is parked at the health check that gates the rotation, so healthWait()
// returning Stopping just makes readyToRotate false. run() skips the
// rotation body, comes back around to the top of its loop, and returns
// from there after clearing working_. Contrast
// testHealthWaitStopMidRotation(), where run() returns from inside the
// rotation body and leaves working_ set, so an untimed rendezvous()
// there would block forever.
```

* At `:1685-1688` you could actually go further: since `working_` does get cleared on this path, adding `BEAST_EXPECT(store.rendezvous());` after `store.stop()` would be safe there and would assert the clean-shutdown path. That is optional, not required.

