It is equivalent. But it's not obvious because of `if (depth >= kMaxAssetCheckDepth)` check in `checkVaultPseudoAccountFrozenPreconditions()`, which could return `true` (though it's unreachable). I can make this change:

   if (!issuanceSle)
   {
        return std::ranges::any_of(accounts, [&](auto const& account) {
            return isVaultPseudoAccountFrozen(view, account, mptIssue, depth);
        });
    }

Which is going to make it clearly equivalent at the expense of reading issuance SLE for each account. Another option is to make this change:

    if (!issuanceSle)
        return view.rules().enabled(featureSingleAssetVault) && depth >= kMaxAssetCheckDepth;

Or keep as is and add some clarifying comments:

    // Bypassing `isVaultPseudoAccountFrozen` also bypasses its
    // `depth >= kMaxAssetCheckDepth` fallback, which cannot matter here:
    // depth accrues only via vault-share hops, and those cannot nest
    // (VaultCreate rejects assets issued by a pseudo-account).
    if (!issuanceSle)
        return false;

What do you think?

I think it is better to keep it explicit, 

```
if (!issuanceSle)
{
    return std::ranges::any_of(accounts, [&](auto const& account) {
        return isVaultPseudoAccountFrozen(view, account, mptIssue, depth);
    });
}

return std::ranges::any_of(accounts, [&](auto const& account) {
    return isVaultPseudoAccountFrozen(view, account, *issuanceSle, depth);
});
```
